"CSS Color Module Level 4 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color-4/)\nciteturn1379view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1377view0\",\"lineno\":2430}); Total lines: 5518\nL2403:  hsl(0 0% 50%) and  hsl(200 0% 50%) are cite1129†equivalent colors , by either of two routes. Their saturation is 0%, so at step 1 the hue of each becomes a cite377†missing component , and a missing component is equal to another missing component. Independently, cite355†hsl() is considered to be in the cite179†srgb cite1130†<color-space> , where the hue is not one of the components being compared at all.\nL2404:  cite384†white and  hwb(120 100% 0%) are cite1129†equivalent colors . Whiteness and blackness sum to 100, so the hue is cite383†powerless and becomes cite377†missing at step 1; and since cite357†hwb() is considered to be in the cite179†srgb cite1130†<color-space> , both colors are compared as R, G and B of 100%.\nL2405:  oklch(50% 0 40) and  oklch(50% 0 200) are cite1129†equivalent colors . The chroma is zero, so each hue is cite383†powerless and becomes cite377†missing at step 1, leaving two colors whose every component compares as equal.\nL2406:  lch(50% 0.001 30) and  lch(50% 0.001 200) are cite1129†equivalent colors , even though their chroma is not exactly zero and their hues are 170 degrees apart. A chroma of 0.001 is within the ε of 0.0015 that cite359†lch() defines for a cite383†powerless hue, so at step 1 both hues become cite377†missing and both chromas are set to zero.\nL2407:  lch(50% 30 0) and  lch(50% 30 360) are cite1129†equivalent colors , but not because of anything this algorithm does: a cite352†<hue> is normalized to the range [0, 360) when it is parsed, so by the time the two are compared both hue components are 0.\nL2408:  rgb(none 128 0) and  rgb(0 128 0) are not cite1129†equivalent colors , although they render identically, since a cite377†missing component behaves as zero when a color is displayed. For the purposes of this comparison a missing component is only ever equal to another missing component, never to a component whose value happens to be zero.\nL2409:  oklch(50% 0 none) and  oklab(50% 0 0) are not cite1129†equivalent colors , despite being colorimetrically identical. They are in different cite1130†<color-space> s, and the first has a cite377†missing component , so step 3 returns false without any component values being compared.\nL2410: \nL2411:  cite692†red and  color(srgb-linear 1 0 0) are cite1129†equivalent colors . They are in different cite1130†<color-space> s and neither has a cite377†missing component , so step 4 converts both to cite910†oklab , where they have the same value.\nL2412:  color(display-p3 1 1 1) and  cite384†white are cite1129†equivalent colors . Converting each to cite910†oklab at step 4 leaves component differences of the order of 10^{-16}, which is why that step compares with an ε rather than for exact equality.\nL2413:  lab(50% 0 0) and  oklab(0.5 0 0) are not cite1129†equivalent colors . Both are achromatic with a lightness of 50%, but the two lightness scales are not the same: converted to cite910†oklab at step 4 their lightness components are 0.56897 and 0.5, which differ by far more than the standardized ε.\nL2414: ## 13. Color Interpolation\nL2415: \nL2416: Color interpolation happens with gradients, compositing, filters, transitions, animations, and color mixing and color modification functions.\nL2417: \nL2418: Interpolation between two cite17†<color> values takes place by executing the following steps:\nL2419:   1. checking the two colors for cite443†analogous components and cite1134†analogous sets which will be carried forward\nL2420:   2. (if conversion is required) changing any cite383†powerless components to cite377†missing values\nL2421:   3. (if required) converting them both to a given color space which will be referred to as the interpolation color space below.\nL2422:   4. (if required) re-inserting cite1135†carried forward values in the converted colors\nL2423:   5. fill in missing components with the other color’s component values\nL2424:   6. (if required) fixing up the hues, depending on the selected cite1136†<hue-interpolation-method> L2425:   7. changing the color components to cite1137†premultiplied form\nL2426:   8. linearly interpolating each component of the computed value of the color separately\nL2427:   9. undoing cite1137†premultiplication L2428: Interpolating to or from cite226†currentcolor is possible. The numerical value used for this purpose is the used value.\nL2429: ### 13.1. Component-wise Linear Interpolation\nL2430: \nL2431: There are two ways to conceptualize how much each color component contributes to the final result: cite1138†color quantities and cite1139†color progress . Both give the same result; they are just different mental models.\nL2432: \nL2433: For example, when mixing the lightness components of two colors `La` and `Lb`, the natural mental model is that of a pair of quantities: I want 70% of `La` and 30% of `Lb`.\nL2434: For example, when animating the lightness component of a color, from a starting value of `La` to en ending value of `Lb`, the natural mental model is of a single progress value which is 0% at the start of the animation and 100% at the end of the animation. At a progress of 0%, the interpolated component is equal to 100% `La` and 0% of `Lb`; at a progress of 100%, the interpolated component is equal to 0% `La` and 100% of `Lb`.\nL2435: Thus, at a progress of 30%, the interpolated component is equal to 70% of `La` and 30% of `Lb`.\nL2436: color quantities\nL2437:     Given a pair of percentage quantities `Qa` and `Qb`, which have been normalized to add to 100%, and a pair of color component values `Ca` and `Cb`, the interpolated result is (`Ca` × `Qa`) + ( `Cb` × `Qb`).\nL2438: color progress\nL2439:     Given a percentage progress `P` and the starting and ending component values `Cs` and `Ce`, the interpolated result is (`Cs` × (1 - `P`) + `Ce` × `P`).\nL2440: \nL2441: Thus, when converting from one model to another, the progress is the amount of the second color.\nL2442: ### 13.2. Color Space for Interpolation\nL2443: \nL2444: Various features in CSS depend on interpolating colors.\nL2445: \nL2446: Examples include:\nL2447: \nL2448:   * cite1140†<gradient> L2449: \nL2450:   * cite1141†filter L2451: \nL2452:   * cite1142†animation L2453: \nL2454:   * cite1143†transition L2455: \nL2456:   * cite439†color-mix() L2457: \nL2458:   * cite1144†relative color syntax\nL2459: \nL2460: To interpolate, the colors must be in (or be converted to) the same color space.\nL2461: Mixing or otherwise combining colors has different results depending on the cite1145†interpolation color space used. Thus, different color spaces may be more appropriate for each interpolation use case.\nL2462: \nL2463:   * In some cases, the result of physically mixing two colored lights is desired. In that case, the CIE cite923†XYZ , cite920†display-p3-linear or cite919†srgb-linear color spaces are appropriate, because they are linear in light intensity.\nL2464:   * If colors need to be evenly spaced perceptually (such as in a gradient), the cite910†Oklab color space (and to a lesser extent, the older cite824†Lab ), are designed to be perceptually uniform.\nL2465: \nL2466:   * If avoiding graying out in color mixing is desired, i.e. maximizing chroma throughout the transition, cite911†OkLCh (and to a lesser extent, the older cite825†LCH ) work well for that.\nL2467:   * Lastly, compatibility with legacy Web content may be the most important consideration. The cite179†sRGB color space, which is neither linear-light nor perceptually uniform, is the choice here, even though it produces poorer results (overly dark or greyish mixes).\nL2468: \nL2469: These features are collectively termed the host syntax.\nL2470: To permit a host syntax to indicate the cite1145†interpolation color space , this specification exports a cite1146†color-interpolation-method production. It is not used by this specification itself, only exposed so that other specifications can use it; see e.g. use in cite1147†CSS Images 4 § 3.1 Linear Gradients: the linear-gradient() notation .\nL2471: The host syntax should define what the default cite1145†interpolation color space should be for each case, and preferably provide syntax for authors to override this default. If such syntax is part of a property value, it should use the cite1146†color-interpolation-method production, defined below for easy reference from other specifications. This ensures consistency across CSS, and that further customizations on how color interpolation is performed can automatically percolate across all of CSS.\nL2472:     <color-space> = <rectangular-color-space> cite297†| <polar-color-space>\nL2473:     <rectangular-color-space> = cite179†srgb cite297†| cite919†srgb-linear cite297†| cite178†display-p3 cite297†| cite920†display-p3-linear cite297†| cite921†a98-rgb cite297†| cite913†prophoto-rgb cite297†| cite914†rec2020 cite297†| cite824†lab cite297†| cite910†oklab cite297†| cite922†<xyz-space> L2474:     <polar-color-space> = cite442†hsl cite297†| cite444†hwb cite297†| cite825†lch cite297†| cite911†oklch L2475:     <hue-interpolation-method> = [ shorter cite297†| longer cite297†| increasing cite297†| decreasing ] hue\nL2476:     <color-interpolation-method> = in [ cite1148†<rectangular-color-space> cite297†| cite1149†<polar-color-space> cite1136†<hue-interpolation-method> cite458†? ]\nL2477: The keywords in the definitions of cite1148†<rectangular-color-space> and cite1149†<polar-color-space> each refer to their corresponding color space, represented in CSS either by the functional syntax with the same name, or (if no such function is present), by the corresponding cite552†<ident> in the cite362†color() function.\nL2478: \nL2479: Tests\nL2480:   * cite1150†color-mix-percents-01.html†wpt.fyi cite1151†(live test)†wpt.live cite1152†(source)†github.com L2481:   * cite1153†color-mix-percents-02.html†wpt.fyi cite1154†(live test)†wpt.live cite1155†(source)†github.com L2482:   * cite1156†gradients-with-transparent.html†wpt.fyi cite1157†(live test)†wpt.live cite1158†(source)†github.com L2483:   * cite1159†gradient-eval-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1160†(live test)†wpt.live cite1161†(source)†github.com L2484:   * cite1162†gradient-eval-002.html†wpt.fyi cite1163†(live test)†wpt.live cite1164†(source)†github.com L2485:   * cite1165†gradient-eval-003.html†wpt.fyi cite1166†(live test)†wpt.live cite1167†(source)†github.com L2486:   * cite1168†gradient-eval-004.html†wpt.fyi cite1169†(live test)†wpt.live cite1170†(source)†github.com L2487:   * cite1171†gradient-eval-005.html†wpt.fyi cite1172†(live test)†wpt.live cite1173†(source)†github.com L2488:   * cite1174†gradient-eval-006.html†wpt.fyi cite1175†(live test)†wpt.live cite1176†(source)†github.com L2489:   * cite1177†gradient-eval-007.html†wpt.fyi cite1178†(live test)†wpt.live cite1179†(source)†github.com L2490:   * cite1180†gradient-eval-008.html†wpt.fyi cite1181†(live test)†wpt.live cite1182†(source)†github.com L2491:   * cite1183†gradient-eval-009.html†wpt.fyi cite1184†(live test)†wpt.live cite1185†(source)†github.com L2492:   * cite1186†gradient-none-interpolation.html†wpt.fyi cite1187†(live test)†wpt.live cite1188†(source)†github.com L2493:   * cite1189†oklab-gradient.html†wpt.fyi cite1190†(live test)†wpt.live cite1191†(source)†github.com L2494:   * cite1192†srgb-gradient.html†wpt.fyi cite1193†(live test)†wpt.live cite1194†(source)†github.com L2495:   * cite1195†srgb-linear-gradient.html†wpt.fyi cite1196†(live test)†wpt.live cite1197†(source)†github.com L2496:   * cite1198†xyz-gradient.html†wpt.fyi cite1199†(live test)†wpt.live cite1200†(source)†github.com L2497:   * cite1201†gradient-interpolation-method-valid.html†wpt.fyi cite1202†(live test)†wpt.live cite1203†(source)†github.com L2498:   * cite1204†gradient-interpolation-method-invalid.html†wpt.fyi cite1205†(live test)†wpt.live cite1206†(source)†github.com L2499:   * cite1207†gradient-interpolation-method-computed.html†wpt.fyi cite1208†(live test)†wpt.live cite1209†(source)†github.com L2500: If the host syntax does not define what color space interpolation should take place in, it defaults to Oklab.\nL2501: \nL2502: For a cite1149†<polar-color-space> if the cite1136†<hue-interpolation-method> is not specified, it defaults to cite1210†shorter .\nL2503: However, user agents must handle interpolation between legacy sRGB color formats (hex colors, named colors, cite353†rgb() , cite355†hsl() or cite357†hwb() and the equivalent alpha-including forms) in gamma-encoded sRGB space. This provides Web compatibility; legacy sRGB content interpolates in the sRGB space by default.\nL2504: \nL2505: Tests\nL2506: \nL2507:   * cite1211†legacy-color-gradient.html†wpt.fyi cite1212†(live test)†wpt.live cite1213†(source)†github.com L2508: This also means that authors can choose to opt-in to better interpolation, even between sRGB colors, by using the non-legacy color(srgb r g b) form for at least one of their colors, or by explicitly specifying an cite1145†interpolation color space .\nL2509: \nL2510: Tests\nL2511: \nL2512:   * cite1214†css-color-4-colors-default-to-oklab-gradient.html†wpt.fyi cite1215†(live test)†wpt.live cite1216†(source)†github.com L2513: If the colors to be interpolated are outside the gamut of the cite1145†interpolation color space , then once converted to that space, they will contain out of range values.\nL2514: \nL2515: These are not clipped; the values must be interpolated as-is.\nL2516: ### 13.3. Interpolating with Missing Components\nL2517: \nL2518: In the course of converting the two colors to the cite1145†interpolation color space , any cite377†missing components would be replaced with the value 0.\nL2519: Thus, the first stage in interpolating two colors is to classify any cite377†missing components in the input colors, and compare them to the components of the cite1145†interpolation color space . If any cite443†analogous components which are missing components are found, they will be carried forward and re-inserted in the converted color before premultiplication, and before linear interpolation takes place.\nL2520: Similarly, if every component of an cite1134†analogous set (defined below) in the original color is a cite377†missing component , they are all cite1135†carried forward and re-inserted in the corresponding analogous set of the cite1145†interpolation color space .\nL2521: \nL2522: Alpha is its own analogous component (alpha is analogous to alpha), so a cite377†missing alpha is cite1135†carried forward in exactly the same way as any other missing component.\nL2523: \nL2524: The analogous components are as follows:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Fonts Module Level 4 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-fonts-4/)\nciteturn1379view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1377view6\",\"lineno\":2090}); Total lines: 4953\nL2063:     4. cite663†font-size must be matched within a UA-dependent margin of tolerance. (Typically, sizes for scalable fonts are rounded to the nearest whole pixel, while the tolerance for bitmapped fonts could be as large as 20%.) Further computations, e.g., by cite724†em values in other properties, are based on the font-size value that is used, not the one that is specified.\nL2064: Note that more than one font might be remaining in the matching set after performing the above steps. If so, the user agent must choose a single font from the matching set and continue these steps with it. The choice of which font to choose can differ between multiple user agents and multiple operating system platforms; however, it must not differ between two elements in the same document.\nL2065:   5. If the matched face is defined via cite979†@font-face rules, user agents must use the procedure below to select a single font:\nL2066: \nL2067:     1. If the font resource has not been loaded and the range of characters defined by the cite1118†unicode-range descriptor value includes the character in question, load the font.\nL2068: \nL2069:     2. After downloading, if the cite1273†effective character map supports the character in question, select that font.\nL2070: When the matched face is a cite393†composite face , user agents must use the procedure above on each of the faces in the composite face in reverse order of cite979†@font-face rule definition.\nL2071: \nL2072: While the download occurs, user agents must either wait until the font is downloaded or render once with substituted font metrics and render again once the font is downloaded.\nL2073:   6. If no matching face exists or the matched face does not contain a glyph for the character to be rendered, the next family name is selected and the previous three steps repeated. Glyphs from other faces in the family are not considered. The only exception is that user agents may optionally substitute a synthetically obliqued version of the cite1274†default face if that face supports a given glyph and synthesis of these faces is permitted by the value of the cite183†font-synthesis property.\nL2074: For example, a synthetic italic version of the regular face might be used if the italic face doesn’t support glyphs for Arabic.\nL2075:   7. If there are no more font families to be evaluated and no matching face has been found, then the user agent performs an installed font fallback procedure to find the best match for the character to be rendered. The result of this procedure can vary across user agents.\nL2076:   8. If a particular character cannot be displayed using any font, the user agent should indicate by some means that a character is not being displayed, displaying either a symbolic representation of the missing glyph (e.g. using a cite1275†Last Resort Font†en.wikipedia.org ) or using the missing character glyph from a default font.\nL2077: Optimizations of this process are allowed provided that an implementation behaves as if the algorithm had been followed exactly. Matching occurs in a well-defined order to ensure that the results are as consistent as possible across user agents, given an identical set of available fonts and rendering tech.\nL2078: The first available font, used for example in the definition of cite184†font-relative lengths such as cite1276†ex or in the definition of the cite182†line-height property, is defined to be the first font for which the character U+0020 (space) is not excluded by a cite1118†unicode-range , given the font families in the cite185†font-family list (or a user agent’s default font if none are available).\nL2079: Installed fonts referenced directly by family name, rather than via @font-face rules, are considered to have a cite1118†unicode-range that covers the entire Unicode code space.\nL2080: \nL2081: Tests\nL2082:   * cite1277†first-available-font-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1278†(live test)†wpt.live cite1279†(source)†github.com L2083:   * cite1280†first-available-font-002.html†wpt.fyi cite1281†(live test)†wpt.live cite1282†(source)†github.com L2084:   * cite1283†first-available-font-003.html†wpt.fyi cite1284†(live test)†wpt.live cite1285†(source)†github.com L2085:   * cite1286†first-available-font-004.html†wpt.fyi cite1287†(live test)†wpt.live cite1288†(source)†github.com L2086:   * cite1289†first-available-font-005.html†wpt.fyi cite1290†(live test)†wpt.live cite1291†(source)†github.com L2087:   * cite1292†first-available-font-006.html†wpt.fyi cite1293†(live test)†wpt.live cite1294†(source)†github.com L2088:   * cite1295†first-available-font-007.html†wpt.fyi cite1296†(live test)†wpt.live cite1297†(source)†github.com L2089:   * cite1298†italic-oblique-fallback.html†wpt.fyi cite1299†(live test)†wpt.live cite1300†(source)†github.com L2090: Note: it does not matter whether that font actually has a glyph for the space character.\nL2091: ### 5.3. Cluster matching\nL2092: When text contains characters such as combining marks, ideally the base character should be rendered using the same font as the mark, this assures proper placement of the mark. For this reason, the font matching algorithm for clusters is more specialized than the general case of matching a single character by itself.\nL2093: For sequences containing variation selectors, which indicate the precise glyph to be used for a given character, user agents always attempt cite1301†installed font fallback to find the appropriate glyph before using the default glyph of the base character.\nL2094: A sequence of codepoints containing combining mark or other modifiers is termed a grapheme cluster (see cite1302†[CSS3TEXT] and cite1303†[UAX29] for a more complete description). For a given cluster containing a base character, b and a sequence of combining characters c1, c2…, the entire cluster is matched using these steps:\nL2095:   1. For each family in the font list, a face is chosen using the style selection rules defined in the previous section.\nL2096:     1. If all characters in the sequence b + c1 + c2 … are completely supported by the font, select this font for the sequence.\nL2097:     2. If a sequence of multiple codepoints is canonically equivalent to a single character and the font cite1304†supports that character, select this font for the sequence and use the glyph associated with the canonically equivalent character for the entire cluster.\nL2098:   2. If no font was found in the font list in step 1:\nL2099:     1. If c1 is a variation selector, system fallback must be used to find a font that supports the full sequence of b + c1. If no font on the system cite1304†supports the full sequence, match the single character b using the normal procedure for matching single characters and ignore the variation selector. Note: a sequence with more than one variation selector must be treated as an encoding error and the trailing selectors must be ignored. cite1241†[UNICODE] L2100:     2. Otherwise, the user agent may optionally use installed font fallback to match a font that cite1304†supports the entire cluster.\nL2101:   3. If no font is found in step 2, use the matching sequence from step 1 to determine the longest sequence that is completely cite1304†supported by a font in the font list and attempt to match the remaining combining characters separately using the rules for single characters.\nL2102: ### 5.4. Character handling issues\nL2103: \nL2104: CSS font matching is always performed on text runs containing Unicode characters cite1241†[UNICODE] , so documents using legacy encodings are assumed to have been transcoded before matching fonts. For fonts containing cite662†character maps for both legacy encodings and Unicode, the contents of the legacy encoding character map must have no effect on the results of the font matching process.\nL2105: The font matching process does not assume that text runs are in either normalized or denormalized form (see cite1305†[CHARMOD-NORM] for more details). Fonts may only support precomposed forms and not the decomposed sequence of base character plus combining marks. Authors should always tailor their choice of fonts to their content, including whether that content contains normalized or denormalized character streams.\nL2106: If a given character is a Private-Use Area Unicode codepoint, user agents must only match font families named in the cite185†font-family list that are not generic families. If none of the families named in the font-family list contain a glyph for that codepoint, user agents must display some form of missing glyph symbol for that character rather than attempting cite1301†installed font fallback for that codepoint.\nL2107: When matching the replacement character U+FFFD, user agents may skip the font matching process and immediately display some form of missing glyph symbol, they are not required to display the glyph from the font that would be selected by the font matching process.\nL2108: In general, the fonts for a given family will all have the same or similar cite662†character maps . The process outlined here is designed to handle even font families containing faces with widely variant character maps. However, authors are cautioned that the use of such families can lead to unexpected results.\nL2109: \nL2110: Tests\nL2111:   * cite1306†font-unicode-PUA.html†wpt.fyi cite1307†(live test)†wpt.live cite1308†(source)†github.com L2112:   * cite1309†font-unicode-PUA-primary-font.html†wpt.fyi cite1310†(live test)†wpt.live cite1311†(source)†github.com L2113: ## 6. Font Feature Properties\nL2114: Modern font technologies support a variety of advanced typographic and language-specific font features. Using these features, a single font can provide glyphs for a wide range of ligatures, contextual and stylistic alternates, tabular and old-style figures, small capitals, automatic fractions, swashes, and alternates specific to a given language. To allow authors control over these font capabilities, the cite846†font-variant property has been expanded.\nL2115: It now functions as a shorthand for a set of properties that provide control over stylistic font features.\nL2116: ### 6.1. Glyph selection and positioning\nL2117: \nL2118: This section is non-normative\nL2119: Simple fonts used for displaying Latin text use a very basic processing model. Fonts contain a cite662†character map which maps each character to a glyph for that character. Glyphs for subsequent characters are simply placed one after the other along a run of text. Modern font formats such as OpenType and AAT (Apple Advanced Typography) use a richer processing model.\nL2120: The glyph for a given character can be chosen and positioned not just based on the codepoint of the character itself, but also on adjacent characters as well as the language, script, and features enabled for the text. Font features may be required for specific scripts, or recommended as enabled by default or they might be stylistic features meant to be used under author control.\nL2121: The point at which font selection and positioning happens in the overall order of text processing operations (such as text transformation, text orientation and text alignment) is described in cite1312†CSS Text 3 § A Text Processing Order of Operations .\nL2122: For a detailed description of glyph processing for OpenType fonts, see cite1313†[WINDOWS-GLYPH-PROC] .\nL2123: \nL2124: Stylistic font features can be classified into two broad categories: ones that affect the harmonization of glyph shapes with the surrounding context, such as kerning and ligature features, and ones such as the small-caps, subscript/superscript and alternate features that affect shape selection.\nL2125: The subproperties of cite846†font-variant listed below are used to control these stylistic font features. They do not control features that are required for displaying certain scripts, such as the OpenType features used when displaying Arabic or Indic language text. They affect glyph selection and positioning, but do not affect font selection as described in the font matching section (except in cases required for compatibility with CSS 2.1).\nL2126: To assure consistent behavior across user agents, the equivalent OpenType property settings are listed for individual properties and are normative. When using other font formats these should be used as a guideline to map CSS font feature property values to specific font features.\nL2127: ### 6.2. Language-specific display\nL2128: OpenType also supports language-specific glyph selection and positioning, so that text can be displayed correctly in cases where the language dictates a specific display behavior. Many languages share a common script, but the shape of certain letters can vary across those languages. For example, certain Cyrillic letters have different shapes in Russian text than in Bulgarian. In Latin text, it’s common to render \"fi\" with an explicit fi-ligature that lacks a dot on the \"i\".\nL2129: However, in languages such as Turkish which uses both a dotted-i and a dotless-i, it’s important to not use this ligature or use a specialized version that contains a dot over the \"i\".\nL2130: Tests\nL2131: \nL2132:   * language-specific-01.html (visual test) cite1314†(source)†github.com L2133:   * cite1315†mvs-shaping.html†wpt.fyi cite1316†(live test)†wpt.live cite1317†(source)†github.com L2134: \nL2135: The example below shows language-specific variations based on stylistic traditions found in Spanish, Italian and French orthography:\nL2136: \nL2137: cite1318†Image: language specific forms, spanish L2138: \nL2139: cite1319†Image: language specific forms, italian L2140: \nL2141: cite1320†Image: language specific forms, french L2142: If the content language of the element is known according to the rules of the cite1321†document language†www.w3.org , user agents are required to infer the OpenType language system from the cite394†content language and use that when selecting and positioning glyphs using an OpenType font. If a writing system has been explicitly specified, it must take precedence over the customary one implied by the content language.\n-----